{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's cross-city mechanism is physically inconsistent with the per-unit-area quantity it is asked to explain. The observed nocturnal UHI is a temperature *difference* (°C) measured per location, not a total heat content (J). The target justifies the 'larger gap in larger cities' by saying a larger contiguous impervious area generates a 'deeper and more persistent volume of warm air' (i.e., greater *total* stored heat and a larger atmospheric volume). But accumulating more total heat over more square kilometers does not raise the temperature at any individual point; unless additional heat is concentrated per unit area, the excess merely distributes over a proportionally larger volume/area, leaving the point temperature unchanged. Thus the stated mechanism predicts total urban heat content, not a higher nocturnal temperature gap. The counterconditions also concede this flaw: 'If per-unit-area heat storage does not scale with total city area' would refute the claim, yet the mechanism provided never demonstrates such per-unit-area scaling; it only scales total impervious area with total extent. A known stronger single modulator the problem asks for is the sky view factor / canyon geometry, which directly slows nocturnal longwave cooling per unit area regardless of city size.","counterexample":["A uniformly low-albedo, high-thermal-mass asphalt surface of 10 km² vs 1000 km² under the same solar forcing and same nocturnal cooling rate: the larger city stores ~100x more total heat, but the surface temperature at the center (and thus the local UHI temperature gap) is essentially identical in both, because the stored heat per unit area and the longwave cooling loss per unit area are identical. City size alone does not increase the intensive temperature gap without a per-unit-area mechanism."],"premise":"The problem asks why the nocturnal temperature *gap* (a per-unit-area intensive quantity, °C) is larger in some cities, and for the single mechanism that best explains that cross-city variation.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"4dd62735b90864a75220e09b302500bc","quote":"what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others"}],"target":"3259581ef13b8d83f6789eea95e14a3e91cfc6a780dc720f8acecc5d8d5d5444"}]}